# Microsoft Office lock files.
#
# Word and PowerPoint write a `~$name.docx` beside a document for as long as
# it is open, holding the editor's name so a second person gets told who has
# it. They are per-machine scratch files and mean nothing to anyone else.
#
# One was committed by accident. The pattern is deliberately unanchored, so it
# applies at every depth: the documents in this repository sit both at the
# root and under Product-Description/.
~$*

# The Hinglish twin of PROJECT-GUIDE.md. Deliberately not committed: this is a
# product other companies buy and run, and its shipped documentation stays in
# one language. The file is for reading here, not for shipping — and it must be
# updated in step with PROJECT-GUIDE.md whenever the project changes.
PROJECT-GUIDE.hinglish.md

# Scratch from the local browser and slide tooling: Chrome profiles,
# screenshots, and a node_modules install of its own. One `git add -A` away
# from being in the history permanently, where it cannot practically be
# removed again.
output/
.ppt-build/

# The generated screen-flow poster. Built from the screenshots under output/
# by scripts/build-live-screenshot-flow-ppt.mjs, so it is an artifact of a
# script that is committed rather than a document anybody edits — 12 MB of it,
# rebuilt every time the screens change.
#
# Named rather than `*.pptx`, deliberately: the hand-made decks in this
# repository are tracked, and a blanket pattern would quietly keep the next
# one out of a commit somebody meant to make.
UBOSS_Sourcing_Screen_Flow_Poster_2026.pptx

# Logs from scripts/dev-stack.ps1. Each of the five processes it starts is
# detached with its stdout and stderr redirected here, because a hidden window
# has nowhere else to put them - and those files are the whole reason a crash
# on boot is diagnosable rather than just a Vite proxy error. Per-machine
# scratch, and they contain local connection strings and seed credentials.
.dev-logs/

# Environment files.
#
# `backend/.env` holds every secret this system has: the database password, the
# payment gateway keys, the token-signing secrets and the key that decrypts
# customers' stored ERP credentials. It has never been committed - but nothing
# was stopping it, and it sits in the working tree one `git add -A` away from
# being in the history permanently, where it cannot practically be removed.
# The same goes for the `.env.before-*` copies left behind when a feature added
# new settings.
#
# So the default is to ignore every environment file, and the exceptions are
# named rather than assumed:
#
#   .env.example    The template. Placeholders only, and the thing every
#                   deployment starts from - it has to be committed.
#   apps/*/.env     The three frontends. These hold ONE setting,
#                   VITE_API_BASE_URL, pointing at localhost for development.
#                   Not a secret, and committed on purpose so a fresh clone
#                   runs. Production does NOT use them: deploy/scripts/
#                   release.sh exports VITE_API_BASE_URL at build time from
#                   the real public URL, because Vite bakes this value into
#                   the bundle and a released build that kept the localhost
#                   default would load perfectly and talk to nobody.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
!apps/*/.env
